WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.150
<v Speaker 1>Welcome in, everyone.

2
00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:07.390
<v Speaker 1>We are inviting you to look through the lens of the classical Ismaili reading as we explore a really profound text.

3
00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:12.750
<v Speaker 1>We're looking at the treatise establishing the I Am Umate by Anay Saburi.

4
00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:20.110
<v Speaker 1>He now asks what the summit of creation actually authorizes and answers with his boldest claims about the Imam's dominion.

5
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.710
<v Speaker 2>All was made for the Imam.

6
00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:25.310
<v Speaker 2>His spirit set his reach and the world's head must be its best.

7
00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:32.830
<v Speaker 1>That exact sentence, I mean, it captures A profound and honestly pretty uncomfortable crisis that we really have to unpack.

8
00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:33.910
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, it definitely does.

9
00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:41.830
<v Speaker 1>Because to establish where this whole conversation begins, we have to look at the massive, intricate ladder of creation the author has built.

10
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:47.710
<v Speaker 1>He envisions this strictly ordered universe, you know, where absolutely nothing exists in isolation.

11
00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:53.790
<v Speaker 1>He asks you to look at the bare earth, just the base elements, and those elements rise and are refined into metals.

12
00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.350
<v Speaker 1>Like the earth yields gold, it yields silver.

13
00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.870
<v Speaker 1>It yields the unburnable ruby hidden deep down inside the rock.

14
00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.670
<v Speaker 2>But crucially, the rock doesn't actually know it holds a ruby.

15
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:03.310
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

16
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:04.910
<v Speaker 1>And then the ladder moves up again.

17
00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.870
<v Speaker 1>The metals and the elements rise into plant life.

18
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.830
<v Speaker 1>You get the balanced grain of the fields, the fruit-bearing trees.

19
00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.950
<v Speaker 1>But again, the tree has no idea it provides shade.

20
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:16.670
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, there's no consciousness of its own utility.

21
00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:16.990
<v Speaker 1>Right.

22
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.430
<v Speaker 1>I mean, exactly.

23
00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:21.150
<v Speaker 1>Then the plants rise into the animal kingdom.

24
00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:24.510
<v Speaker 1>The grazing herds, the beasts of burden, the predators.

25
00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:27.630
<v Speaker 1>And finally, the animals rise into humanity.

26
00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.990
<v Speaker 1>So 2 absolute conclusions sit locked together at the very top of that climb.

27
00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:33.550
<v Speaker 2>Which are.

28
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:37.390
<v Speaker 1>Well, first, that humanity is the summit of the whole lower creation.

29
00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:41.550
<v Speaker 1>And 2nd, that the absolute summit of humanity is the I am.

30
00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:42.070
<v Speaker 2>Yeah.

31
00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:45.310
<v Speaker 2>And arriving at a summit is a really demanding thing.

32
00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:45.790
<v Speaker 1>How so?

33
00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.830
<v Speaker 2>Well, if you build an architecture of the universe that is just so

34
00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:57.550
<v Speaker 2>strictly hierarchical, so perfectly ordered from the lowest pebble all the way to the highest human soul, that positioning demands a resolution.

35
00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.790
<v Speaker 1>Because you can't just be at the top for no reason.

36
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:00.350
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

37
00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:06.430
<v Speaker 2>Being at the absolute pinnacle means everything beneath you either owes you something or you owe something to it.

38
00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:13.950
<v Speaker 2>The ladder of creation, as beautiful as it is, it leaves this massive question hanging in the air.

39
00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.750
<v Speaker 2>We can grant that the summit has been reached,

40
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:21.430
<v Speaker 2>But what does standing on that summit actually authorize in practice?

41
00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.830
<v Speaker 1>What he's actually allowed to do.

42
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:23.150
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

43
00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:26.190
<v Speaker 2>What may the leader do with the world below him?

44
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:27.310
<v Speaker 2>What may he demand of it?

45
00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:32.990
<v Speaker 2>I mean, what does his position at the apex mean for you and for everyone else just standing on the rungs below?

46
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:37.310
<v Speaker 1>And those questions demand that we look at the very foundation of ownership.

47
00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:38.030
<v Speaker 2>They really do.

48
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:43.310
<v Speaker 1>Because we have to begin with this doctrine of dominion, which asks a rather accusatory question really, like,

49
00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:45.390
<v Speaker 1>Why was any of this made in the 1st place?

50
00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:48.110
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, why does the physical universe exist at all?

51
00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:49.230
<v Speaker 1>It's a massive question.

52
00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:52.510
<v Speaker 2>It is a sweeping question, but the author provides a really sweeping answer.

53
00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.350
<v Speaker 2>You know, look at the stars, the vast oceans, the metals buried in the dark.

54
00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:00.190
<v Speaker 2>Look at the grains swaying in the field, the massive herbs of animals.

55
00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:06.670
<v Speaker 2>The author has established that each lower rung exists purely for the sake of the rung standing just above it.

56
00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.510
<v Speaker 1>The lower creation serves the higher.

57
00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:09.630
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

58
00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.910
<v Speaker 2>And now he closes the chain entirely.

59
00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:16.670
<v Speaker 2>The whole of natural creation was made for the sake of humanity.

60
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.030
<v Speaker 1>Solely for humanity.

61
00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:18.510
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

62
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:28.310
<v Speaker 2>The logic he relies on is that only a being who can possess conscious knowledge and derive conscious articulated benefit gives the rest of the natural world any point whatsoever.

63
00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:28.830
<v Speaker 1>Oh, I see.

64
00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:38.110
<v Speaker 2>Because think about it, a universe full of fruit that is never actually tasted or stars that are never navigated by or beasts that are never utilized.

65
00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:39.510
<v Speaker 1>The universe operating in a total.

66
00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:40.670
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

67
00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:41.910
<v Speaker 2>It's pointless.

68
00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:50.510
<v Speaker 1>So without a human being to eat the fruit or mine the oar or ride the beast or even read the complex movements of the sky to measure time,

69
00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:56.150
<v Speaker 1>There would be 0 wisdom in a single plant or animal ever having been brought into existence.

70
00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:56.990
<v Speaker 2>None whatsoever.

71
00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:59.550
<v Speaker 1>They would just exist in an empty, purposeless loop.

72
00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.670
<v Speaker 1>They need a witness and they need a user just to give their existence a meaning.

73
00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:04.510
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

74
00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.590
<v Speaker 2>But the chain doesn't stop at humanity.

75
00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:07.310
<v Speaker 1>It keeps going.

76
00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:07.950
<v Speaker 2>It does.

77
00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:10.110
<v Speaker 2>Humanity was not made for its own sake either.

78
00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:12.910
<v Speaker 2>Human beings were created for the imams.

79
00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:21.950
<v Speaker 2>Humanity exists to receive religion from them, to be perfected through them, and to manifest their order and foundation in the physical world.

80
00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:26.030
<v Speaker 1>So the worlds exist for humanity and humanity exists for them.

81
00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:26.230
<v Speaker 2>Yeah.

82
00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:35.150
<v Speaker 2>The entire point of everything in the cosmos is finally reached in him, because only in him does the ascent of creation complete itself.

83
00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:38.990
<v Speaker 1>And from that premise, a really startling consequence falls out.

84
00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:39.710
<v Speaker 2>It does.

85
00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:44.110
<v Speaker 1>If all the worlds were ultimately made for him, then all the benefit of those worlds belongs to him.

86
00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:49.150
<v Speaker 1>The rest of creation doesn't stand alongside him as an equal partner in some grand.

87
00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:51.630
<v Speaker 1>cosmic democracy.

88
00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:52.350
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, not at all.

89
00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:59.950
<v Speaker 1>It stands as children stand to a parent, entirely dependent, provided for, owing everything, and owed nothing in return by default.

90
00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.350
<v Speaker 1>So whatever is given back to creation is just a gift of generosity and mercy.

91
00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.670
<v Speaker 1>It's not a debt being repaid.

92
00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.270
<v Speaker 2>Which brings us to a stark, unsoftened reality that the author presents.

93
00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:12.830
<v Speaker 2>Consider the specific image of how an ordinary man looks at an animal.

94
00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.190
<v Speaker 2>This is crucial.

95
00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:16.750
<v Speaker 2>A man walks up to a beast.

96
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:21.870
<v Speaker 2>He can decide on the spot what that animal's life is actually for.

97
00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:25.070
<v Speaker 2>He might decide to ride it to a distant destination.

98
00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:31.150
<v Speaker 2>He might milk it, he might shear it, or he might put it to really hard, exhausting labor in the fields.

99
00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:31.950
<v Speaker 1>Or eat it.

100
00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:32.750
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

101
00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:37.710
<v Speaker 2>He might slaughter it for a feast, or he might simply let it graze peacefully in a meadow.

102
00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:38.590
<v Speaker 2>And he does

103
00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:44.990
<v Speaker 2>all of this entirely by his own judgment, completely against the animal's own inclination or instinct.

104
00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.590
<v Speaker 2>And absolutely no one calls that man a tyrant for doing so.

105
00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.190
<v Speaker 1>Because we just call it the natural order.

106
00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:50.590
<v Speaker 2>We do.

107
00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:53.550
<v Speaker 2>And the author builds his core assertion on this very picture.

108
00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:57.470
<v Speaker 2>He says humanity stands to the imam exactly as the animals stand to humanity.

109
00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:02.430
<v Speaker 1>I don't buy that yet everything belongs to the imam just sounds like a demand for property.

110
00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:03.150
<v Speaker 2>I get that.

111
00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:06.190
<v Speaker 1>Like it just sounds like a philosophical cover for absolute tyranny, you know?

112
00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:10.750
<v Speaker 1>Reducing human beings to livestock whose only purpose is to be used.

113
00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:16.910
<v Speaker 2>It absolutely sounds like sheer exploitation until you examine the underlying mechanics of that dominion.

114
00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:22.750
<v Speaker 2>What the author insists on is that dominion is inherently bound to a strict duty of care.

115
00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:23.870
<v Speaker 1>A duty of care.

116
00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:24.430
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

117
00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.310
<v Speaker 2>The mastery and the care are the exact same relationship.

118
00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:29.630
<v Speaker 2>Just read from two different sides.

119
00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:31.390
<v Speaker 2>Look again at the master and the animal.

120
00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:31.670
<v Speaker 1>Okay.

121
00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.270
<v Speaker 2>A wise master does not merely exploit his beast until it drops dead.

122
00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.790
<v Speaker 2>By the very norms of his rule, he is bound to protect it.

123
00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:40.910
<v Speaker 1>He has to keep it alive.

124
00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:41.630
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

125
00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:43.550
<v Speaker 2>He guards its watering holes.

126
00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.630
<v Speaker 2>He secures its grazing grounds.

127
00:06:45.790 --> 00:06:50.110
<v Speaker 2>He shields it from predators in the night and tends to its well-being when it is sick.

128
00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.870
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, or else he loses the animal.

129
00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:57.790
<v Speaker 2>He even protects the animal against its own foolish appetites, you know, steering it away from poisonous plants it might try to eat.

130
00:06:58.030 --> 00:06:58.510
<v Speaker 1>Oh, I see.

131
00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:01.830
<v Speaker 2>The one who owns the flock is precisely the one obligated to keep it alive.

132
00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.510
<v Speaker 2>Mastery is not an exemption from duty, it is the heaviest duty there is.

133
00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.470
<v Speaker 1>So, the Dominion comes with a permanent, unbreakable charge attached to it.

134
00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:15.630
<v Speaker 1>isn't just the privilege to take, it's the obligation to sustain.

135
00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:16.350
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

136
00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:27.950
<v Speaker 2>The leader is bound by the norms of kingship, the demands of nature, sound policy, and sacred law to protect the welfare of mankind exactly as a man protects the welfare of the animals he governs.

137
00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:32.510
<v Speaker 2>And this duty of care is not some new invention the author just came up with.

138
00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:38.030
<v Speaker 2>It is the beating heart of how the tradition has always described just rule.

139
00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:39.150
<v Speaker 1>There's precedent for it.

140
00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:39.990
<v Speaker 2>Huge precedent.

141
00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:45.950
<v Speaker 2>Consider the instruction given by the commander of the faithful, peace be upon him, when he was dispatching a governor to Egypt.

142
00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:46.630
<v Speaker 1>What did he tell him?

143
00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:50.750
<v Speaker 2>He made mercy the absolute first charge of the office.

144
00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:57.230
<v Speaker 2>He commanded the governor, let your heart be filled with mercy for the subjects and love for them and kindness toward them.

145
00:07:57.550 --> 00:08:01.070
<v Speaker 2>Do not be a ravening beast over them, seizing what they have.

146
00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:03.950
<v Speaker 1>I mean, that's incredibly direct.

147
00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:06.750
<v Speaker 1>So the one set above the flock is bound to tend it.

148
00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:12.710
<v Speaker 1>Turning into a predator over the people is a betrayal of the office, not an exercise of it.

149
00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:16.110
<v Speaker 1>The higher the dominion, the heavier the duty of care bound to it.

150
00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:19.470
<v Speaker 1>If you are the summit, you carry the weight of everything below you.

151
00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:20.350
<v Speaker 2>Carry all of it.

152
00:08:20.510 --> 00:08:24.110
<v Speaker 1>But we still have to ask how this dominion is justified naturally.

153
00:08:24.910 --> 00:08:28.190
<v Speaker 1>Because the author doesn't just rest on theological claims, does he?

154
00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:29.150
<v Speaker 2>No, he doesn't.

155
00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:33.950
<v Speaker 1>He tries to ground this massive authority in the literal laws of biology and nature.

156
00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:34.670
<v Speaker 2>He does.

157
00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:42.030
<v Speaker 2>He returns to that ladder of nature we talked about, but this time he reads it specifically for what it says about power, capacity, and ruling.

158
00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:43.310
<v Speaker 1>Okay, let's break that down.

159
00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:49.550
<v Speaker 2>He asks you to observe how one rung actually rules the rung below it in biological terms.

160
00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:51.310
<v Speaker 2>Look at a plant.

161
00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:51.630
<v Speaker 1>Okay.

162
00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.430
<v Speaker 2>A plant surpasses the bare, lifeless earth

163
00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:58.830
<v Speaker 2>exactly one added thing, the spirit of growth.

164
00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:00.030
<v Speaker 1>Just so that one thing.

165
00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:03.230
<v Speaker 2>That single addition is enough to make the earth completely subservient.

166
00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:05.230
<v Speaker 2>The soil now feeds the root.

167
00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:10.310
<v Speaker 2>The ground becomes something the plant draws its strength from and uses to build its own structure.

168
00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:11.390
<v Speaker 1>That makes perfect sense.

169
00:09:11.390 --> 00:09:16.070
<v Speaker 2>Then you step up a rung, an animal surpasses the plant by one further spirit.

170
00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:16.910
<v Speaker 1>Which is.

171
00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:21.630
<v Speaker 2>The spirit of sensation, the power to feel pain, to desire, and to move voluntarily.

172
00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:30.030
<v Speaker 2>That one addition allows the animal to subdue the plant, walk over the ground, graze on the vegetation, and just consume it entirely at will.

173
00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:38.590
<v Speaker 1>And skipping up again, a human being surpasses the animal by one further spirit, reason, which the text calls N-O-O-T-Q.

174
00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:42.590
<v Speaker 1>This added spirit puts the entire lower world under human hands.

175
00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:51.230
<v Speaker 1>Man sows the earth, bends it to his wisdom, builds fences to corral the animals, rides them, slaughters them, and just uses them as he sees fit.

176
00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:58.270
<v Speaker 1>So at every single level of biology, possessing one added spirit is the entire difference between ruling and being ruled.

177
00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:02.030
<v Speaker 1>The one with the higher spirit always commands the one with the lower.

178
00:10:02.030 --> 00:10:04.030
<v Speaker 2>That is the universal pattern of existence.

179
00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:09.390
<v Speaker 2>And the author simply applies this ironclad biological law to the summit of humanity.

180
00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:11.230
<v Speaker 1>Okay, so what does that look like at the very top?

181
00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:12.870
<v Speaker 2>When the leader takes up his charge,

182
00:10:12.910 --> 00:10:15.390
<v Speaker 2>He is not left dwelling merely on the human rung.

183
00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:21.070
<v Speaker 2>He is endowed with further spirits, further powers, and a purity that no ordinary human being possesses.

184
00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:21.950
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, something extra.

185
00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:23.070
<v Speaker 2>A lot extra.

186
00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:33.630
<v Speaker 2>The choicest of the animal powers, the flawless balance of the elements, the finest influence of the celestial spheres, they all gather in him as the quintessence of the whole world.

187
00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:34.670
<v Speaker 1>Like A culmination.

188
00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:35.470
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

189
00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:40.510
<v Speaker 2>Higher spirits unite in him, culminating in the perfect 7th creative spirit.

190
00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:42.910
<v Speaker 2>The spirit of oneness.

191
00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:43.550
<v Speaker 1>Oh, I see.

192
00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:56.670
<v Speaker 2>So if one single spirit reason was enough to make humanity the absolute master of the beast, the sheer weight of this array of higher, purer spirits establishes absolute dominion over humanity.

193
00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:58.430
<v Speaker 1>So rank isn't just a symbol.

194
00:10:58.430 --> 00:11:01.750
<v Speaker 2>Rank is not merely A symbolic title of authority here.

195
00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:02.990
<v Speaker 2>Rank is authority.

196
00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:09.310
<v Speaker 2>The literal number of spirits residing in the imams physically and naturally determines the extent of their control over humanity.

197
00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:11.190
<v Speaker 1>That is a profound shift in perspective.

198
00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:20.150
<v Speaker 1>Because that dominion is total, grounded in both care and biology, the author arrives at a very heavy conclusion regarding the taking of anything in this physical world.

199
00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:20.990
<v Speaker 2>It really does.

200
00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:30.030
<v Speaker 1>Because if all the benefit of the worlds ultimately belongs to the summit, whoever takes anything from this world without the permission and approval of the leader has committed a forbidden act.

201
00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:33.790
<v Speaker 2>It's not just a discourtesy or a minor infraction.

202
00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:36.510
<v Speaker 2>It's an act of literal usurpation.

203
00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:42.350
<v Speaker 2>It's a seizure of what is not yours to seize from an owner who never granted it to you in the 1st place.

204
00:11:42.350 --> 00:11:44.830
<v Speaker 1>Yes, and he anchors this directly in scripture.

205
00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:51.710
<v Speaker 1>He observes that whatever is bestowed on the messenger from the people of the villages is for God and the messenger.

206
00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:56.190
<v Speaker 2>But the focus there is on the word used for bestowal, isn't it?

207
00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:58.510
<v Speaker 1>The crucial focus is the specific word.

208
00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:00.990
<v Speaker 1>In the tradition, this is what they call FIE.

209
00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:02.590
<v Speaker 2>And what does that mean exactly?

210
00:12:02.830 --> 00:12:05.230
<v Speaker 1>Well, the root meaning of this term is not gift.

211
00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:08.510
<v Speaker 1>It is not spoils of war and it is not tax.

212
00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.590
<v Speaker 1>Its root meaning is return.

213
00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:11.470
<v Speaker 2>Return.

214
00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:11.870
<v Speaker 1>Yeah.

215
00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:16.190
<v Speaker 1>The author points out that Arabic uses this exact same word for a shadow.

216
00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.550
<v Speaker 1>A shadow is called this because in the evening as the sun sets,

217
00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:23.350
<v Speaker 1>The shadow comes back to the pace of the object from which it first stretched out.

218
00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:24.190
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I follow.

219
00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:28.110
<v Speaker 1>Everything in the world comes from the fortune and property of the imams in the 1st place.

220
00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:32.110
<v Speaker 1>So whatever passes into their hands is not being handed over to a stranger.

221
00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:33.230
<v Speaker 1>It is a return.

222
00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:33.750
<v Speaker 2>Like the shadow?

223
00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:37.630
<v Speaker 2>It is a shadow coming home at evening to the object that cast it.

224
00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:42.190
<v Speaker 1>That sounds like wordplay, calling every, taking a return so it can never count as theft.

225
00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:45.630
<v Speaker 2>It looks like an etymological technicality at first glance, I agree.

226
00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:53.390
<v Speaker 2>but it is actually doing massive structural work in the entire argument about the nature of property and rival claims.

227
00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:56.830
<v Speaker 2>Think about how property disputes work.

228
00:12:57.310 --> 00:13:02.750
<v Speaker 2>If the world were originally no ones, if it were just ownerless, neutral ground,

229
00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.510
<v Speaker 2>then the leader's claim on it would just be one claim among many.

230
00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:07.710
<v Speaker 1>You'd have to argue over it.

231
00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:09.110
<v Speaker 2>Right, exactly.

232
00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:12.830
<v Speaker 2>It would have to be weighed against the farmer's claim, the merchant's claim, the citizen's claim.

233
00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:24.510
<v Speaker 2>But if the world was his from the very start, if every good in it is essentially on loan from a source it is always naturally tending back toward, then no rival claim can even get started.

234
00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:25.950
<v Speaker 1>Because there's no neutral ground.

235
00:13:26.030 --> 00:13:30.110
<v Speaker 2>There is simply no unowned ground on which a rival could plant his flag.

236
00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.070
<v Speaker 2>The world does not become his property when he receives it

237
00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:34.830
<v Speaker 2>from you, it was always his.

238
00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:38.190
<v Speaker 1>So receiving it merely makes the ownership visible to us.

239
00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:42.110
<v Speaker 1>It just forces us to acknowledge what was already true in the architecture of the world.

240
00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:42.630
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

241
00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.790
<v Speaker 2>You cannot usurp what is fundamentally unowned.

242
00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:53.470
<v Speaker 2>And once the author establishes that absolutely nothing here is unowned, taking without leave has only one name left.

243
00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:54.190
<v Speaker 1>Theft.

244
00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:54.830
<v Speaker 2>Theft.

245
00:13:55.710 --> 00:14:00.750
<v Speaker 2>Taking an apple from a tree without acknowledging who the tree belongs to is a violation of the cosmic order.

246
00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:06.430
<v Speaker 2>This obligation to defer to the authority is written into the revealed command itself.

247
00:14:07.230 --> 00:14:18.150
<v Speaker 2>The scripture commands the believer to obey God, to obey the prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, and in the very same breath, to obey those in authority among you.

248
00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.830
<v Speaker 1>And he interprets those in authority very specifically.

249
00:14:21.830 --> 00:14:22.190
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

250
00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:30.830
<v Speaker 2>The author reads, those in authority not as a loose reference to any politician or general holding power at a given moment, but specifically as the summit at the top of the ladder.

251
00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:36.750
<v Speaker 2>The world's benefit returning to its rightful owner and the believer's obedience flowing toward that same owner

252
00:14:36.990 --> 00:14:39.710
<v Speaker 2>are two faces of a single unified order.

253
00:14:39.950 --> 00:14:49.310
<v Speaker 1>But if we accept that the leader literally owns the world and that everything is a return to him, that puts a massive psychological burden on the believer.

254
00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:50.270
<v Speaker 2>It really does.

255
00:14:50.350 --> 00:14:57.790
<v Speaker 1>Like how is a person supposed to react when the leader issues a command they don't understand or does something that seems actively harmful?

256
00:14:57.790 --> 00:14:58.910
<v Speaker 2>That's the crisis.

257
00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:04.270
<v Speaker 1>This forces us to examine the posture the believer must adopt, which brings us to the doctrine of wisdom.

258
00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:04.830
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

259
00:15:05.150 --> 00:15:08.430
<v Speaker 2>The term for this specific posture is teslim.

260
00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:10.750
<v Speaker 2>It means a trusting submission.

261
00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:20.270
<v Speaker 2>It's a word that frequently causes modern sensibilities to recoil because it seems to demand an unquestioning blind obedience, you know, just the switching off of the brain.

262
00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:22.670
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, nobody likes feeling like they just have to shut up and obey.

263
00:15:22.670 --> 00:15:23.310
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

264
00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:25.950
<v Speaker 2>The author knows this resistance is deeply natural.

265
00:15:26.110 --> 00:15:28.910
<v Speaker 2>Human beings want to understand what they are doing and why.

266
00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:32.510
<v Speaker 2>So he doesn't begin with a harsh command to simply shut up and obey.

267
00:15:32.830 --> 00:15:34.750
<v Speaker 2>He begins with an image of a farmer.

268
00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:35.950
<v Speaker 1>A farmer.

269
00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:41.150
<v Speaker 2>Picture a man of real agricultural insight walking over a vast tract of land.

270
00:15:41.550 --> 00:15:46.910
<v Speaker 2>He comes upon a stretch of good fertile soil and he knows instantly what its potential is.

271
00:15:47.070 --> 00:15:48.590
<v Speaker 1>He can see it'll grow things well.

272
00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:52.110
<v Speaker 2>Yes, he sees that it will take seed and give back immense growth.

273
00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:54.270
<v Speaker 2>But the land is dry.

274
00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:55.550
<v Speaker 2>So he looks around.

275
00:15:56.030 --> 00:16:03.950
<v Speaker 2>He locates water where others would never think to look, down in distant hidden valleys or buried deep underground beneath layers of rock.

276
00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:09.470
<v Speaker 2>Rather than shrugging and walking on because the task of bringing the water to the soil is incredibly difficult, he labors.

277
00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:17.950
<v Speaker 2>He digs complex canals, he sinks deep wells, and he carefully, meticulously directs that buried water to the wading ground.

278
00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:19.790
<v Speaker 1>Why does he go to such immense trouble though?

279
00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:24.590
<v Speaker 2>Because a man of genuine insight sees no wisdom in leaving good land idle.

280
00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:28.190
<v Speaker 2>He will not let good water remain sealed away in the dark.

281
00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:33.390
<v Speaker 2>It would be a waste and his deep abiding care for the world simply will not tolerate that waste.

282
00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:34.350
<v Speaker 1>I love that image.

283
00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:38.110
<v Speaker 2>The author transposes this exact image directly to the imams.

284
00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:42.750
<v Speaker 2>When they look at people, they do not just see subjects, they see fertile ground.

285
00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:43.910
<v Speaker 1>Oh, that's fascinating.

286
00:16:43.950 --> 00:16:49.470
<v Speaker 2>They see human souls ready, or nearly ready, to receive the seed of wisdom and knowledge.

287
00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:54.510
<v Speaker 2>Even if those specific people are still very far from being able to take it in plainly.

288
00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:56.430
<v Speaker 2>So they dig their canals.

289
00:16:56.750 --> 00:17:03.870
<v Speaker 1>Meaning they labor through preparatory religious exercises, through rigorous disciplines and complex rituals.

290
00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:09.390
<v Speaker 1>And the people subjected to these disciplines often cannot perceive their purpose at the time.

291
00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:13.270
<v Speaker 1>They wonder why they have to perform these specific actions at these specific times.

292
00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:14.190
<v Speaker 2>Of course they do.

293
00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:19.070
<v Speaker 1>But the leaders are patiently channeling toward the soul the water it will one day drink.

294
00:17:19.630 --> 00:17:31.150
<v Speaker 1>And where people simply cannot receive the raw, unvarnished wisdom plainly in open speech because it would overwhelm them or they would reject it entirely, the leaders do what a wise cultivator does with difficult ground.

295
00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:31.790
<v Speaker 2>Which is.

296
00:17:31.870 --> 00:17:34.190
<v Speaker 1>They embed the wisdom in dense symbols.

297
00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:37.790
<v Speaker 1>They place it in obscure words, allegories, and complex actions.

298
00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:42.470
<v Speaker 1>They do this so that people may take the symbol now, hold onto it, practice

299
00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:47.570
<v Speaker 1>and be raised by slow degrees to understand the profound meaning inside it years later.

300
00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:49.890
<v Speaker 1>The obscurity is not an act of withholding.

301
00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:51.490
<v Speaker 1>It is irrigation.

302
00:17:52.130 --> 00:17:58.690
<v Speaker 1>It is wisdom being carefully routed through a channel that the ground can actually absorb without washing away in a flood.

303
00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:03.570
<v Speaker 2>That image of the farmer is the absolute foundation for the submission demanded of the believer.

304
00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:11.730
<v Speaker 2>It becomes strictly necessary to believe in the supreme leadership, to know he is the head of the world and the channel through whom governance reaches creation.

305
00:18:12.130 --> 00:18:12.490
<v Speaker 2>It becomes

306
00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:16.770
<v Speaker 2>necessary never to object to any of his commands, prohibitions, actions, or teachings.

307
00:18:17.170 --> 00:18:25.970
<v Speaker 2>The believer must hold in advance before they understand anything, that all of it embodies absolute wisdom and is strictly for the welfare of creation.

308
00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:31.090
<v Speaker 1>Even when the particular point of a particular thing remains completely hidden for a very long while.

309
00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:32.290
<v Speaker 2>Even then.

310
00:18:32.290 --> 00:18:35.410
<v Speaker 1>The author is deeply realistic about how difficult this is, though.

311
00:18:35.570 --> 00:18:36.290
<v Speaker 2>He is.

312
00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:42.490
<v Speaker 1>The true meaning of a command might be genuinely hard to grasp, and a believer might feel intense internal resistance.

313
00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:51.330
<v Speaker 1>But he frames that resistance as a fact about the believer's limited sight, not a fact about the command's lack of wisdom.

314
00:18:51.650 --> 00:18:52.690
<v Speaker 2>That's the pivot.

315
00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:55.970
<v Speaker 1>He draws a direct parallel to the sacred law itself.

316
00:18:57.170 --> 00:19:00.770
<v Speaker 1>The faithful frequently do not understand what was intended in a certain ruling.

317
00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:03.090
<v Speaker 1>They might bristle at it initially.

318
00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:05.410
<v Speaker 1>They might find a prohibition deeply frustrating.

319
00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:06.370
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, very common.

320
00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:10.690
<v Speaker 1>Only later, when recognition is granted through study or lived experience,

321
00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:15.490
<v Speaker 1>Do they see the profound protective wisdom folded inside it the entire time?

322
00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:25.010
<v Speaker 1>The author is simply asking the believer to extend that exact same patience they already have for the static text of the law to the living guide of the law.

323
00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:28.850
<v Speaker 2>And it extends far beyond the law to the entire natural world too.

324
00:19:29.170 --> 00:19:37.250
<v Speaker 2>People constantly look at creation and see things they deem harmful dangers, pests, natural disasters, things in which they see absolutely no benefit at all.

325
00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:40.810
<v Speaker 1>They rush to conclude no good was ever in them, that the universe made a mistake.

326
00:19:40.930 --> 00:19:41.650
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

327
00:19:41.890 --> 00:19:51.170
<v Speaker 2>Later, the broader ecological or scientific wisdom becomes visible, they grow calm, the objection falls away, and they cannot honestly deny the wisdom they now see.

328
00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.490
<v Speaker 1>The wisdom was not absent, it was just on a delay.

329
00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:54.130
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

330
00:19:54.290 --> 00:20:06.210
<v Speaker 2>Arriving long after the human complaint, you're being asked to grant that your present inability to see the point is merely a fact about the length of your own sight, not about the emptiness of the action itself.

331
00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:10.810
<v Speaker 1>And scripture provides a masterclass on this exact human limitation.

332
00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:11.930
<v Speaker 2>It certainly does.

333
00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:17.810
<v Speaker 1>It's the story of Moses failing this precise test when he set out to learn from his guide.

334
00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:19.410
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, very famous story.

335
00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:30.770
<v Speaker 1>The guide knew Moses' limitations and warned him at the outset that he wouldn't be able to bear what he was about to see, asking how he could have patience for that which he did not encompass in knowledge.

336
00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:34.690
<v Speaker 2>And Moses, despite his prophetic stature, could not maintain that patience.

337
00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:35.490
<v Speaker 1>He really couldn't.

338
00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:40.530
<v Speaker 2>The sequence of events is deeply uncomfortable, even horrifying from an earthly perspective.

339
00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:41.570
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, it's rough.

340
00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:45.090
<v Speaker 2>The guide scuttled a perfectly sound boat endangering the crew.

341
00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:47.330
<v Speaker 2>He killed a young man in cold blood.

342
00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:52.610
<v Speaker 2>He rebuilt a wall in a town that had just cruelly refused them hospitality and food.

343
00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:55.890
<v Speaker 2>And at each and every single act, Moses objected.

344
00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:57.330
<v Speaker 1>Because he couldn't see the reason.

345
00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:03.250
<v Speaker 2>He was entirely unable to hold his peace because all he saw was sheer, senseless damage.

346
00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:08.730
<v Speaker 2>It was only when the guide finally parted from him that the hidden wisdom was disclosed.

347
00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:09.730
<v Speaker 1>What were the reasons?

348
00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:17.250
<v Speaker 2>The boat was scuttled to render it defective and save it from being seized by a tyrant king who was taking every sound ship by force.

349
00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:18.210
<v Speaker 1>Oh, wow.

350
00:21:18.530 --> 00:21:20.610
<v Speaker 1>Oh, I mean, that is a wild twist.

351
00:21:20.610 --> 00:21:20.770
<v Speaker 2>Yeah.

352
00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:26.130
<v Speaker 2>And the young man was taken before he could grow up to commit atrocities and ruin his believing parents.

353
00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:33.570
<v Speaker 2>The wall was raised without payment to guard an orphan's buried treasure from the cruel townspeople until the orphan came of age.

354
00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:35.970
<v Speaker 1>So the wisdom had been there the entire time.

355
00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:38.370
<v Speaker 1>Moses did not lack his own wisdom.

356
00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:42.450
<v Speaker 1>He just lacked the patience to wait for the guide's wisdom to reveal itself.

357
00:21:42.450 --> 00:21:42.930
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

358
00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:51.810
<v Speaker 1>It's A jarring recognition that an act which looks like sheer, senseless damage from below may actually be a profound rescue when seen from above.

359
00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:52.450
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

360
00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:56.290
<v Speaker 1>The distance between damage and rescue is only the length of one's own sight.

361
00:21:56.530 --> 00:22:01.570
<v Speaker 2>This narrative plea for patience then tightens into a rigorous, unforgiving theological proof.

362
00:22:01.650 --> 00:22:03.610
<v Speaker 2>It turns entirely on recognizing

363
00:22:03.730 --> 00:22:04.850
<v Speaker 2>God as the wise one.

364
00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:06.610
<v Speaker 1>Okay, lay out the proof for us.

365
00:22:07.170 --> 00:22:12.290
<v Speaker 2>If you truly grant that the wise one is wise, you have already granted something mathematically massive.

366
00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:17.410
<v Speaker 2>To call someone wise without qualification means everything they do is full of wisdom.

367
00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:19.570
<v Speaker 1>Right, I mean, yes, obviously.

368
00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:23.250
<v Speaker 2>Like they would never, ever do a single thing empty of it.

369
00:22:23.650 --> 00:22:27.010
<v Speaker 2>A futile act is simply impossible for such a being.

370
00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:32.850
<v Speaker 1>And the author is incredibly precise about defining what Fusal means here, isn't he?

371
00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:33.250
<v Speaker 2>Is.

372
00:22:33.650 --> 00:22:42.370
<v Speaker 1>A futile act is 1 where the doing of it and the not doing of it come to exactly the same thing, an act you could have skipped with nothing lost either way.

373
00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:51.250
<v Speaker 1>That kind of arbitrary action is impossible for the wise one because it would mean wisdom would sit in idle and a wisdom that idles

374
00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:53.010
<v Speaker 1>contradicts the very name.

375
00:22:53.490 --> 00:22:55.810
<v Speaker 2>Now watch the hinge of the argument carefully.

376
00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:56.130
<v Speaker 1>Okay.

377
00:22:56.290 --> 00:23:06.530
<v Speaker 2>If it is mathematically impossible for the wise one to perform even one act whose presence and absence are equivalent, how could the leader perform an act where the wisdom lay somewhere else or in the very opposite of what he did?

378
00:23:06.530 --> 00:23:07.130
<v Speaker 1>He couldn't.

379
00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:08.130
<v Speaker 2>He cannot.

380
00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:11.810
<v Speaker 2>His wisdom is not his own personal hard-won human achievement.

381
00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:13.730
<v Speaker 2>It is a direct bestowal.

382
00:23:14.370 --> 00:23:18.690
<v Speaker 2>To act at random without wisdom is to act corruptly and in vain.

383
00:23:19.090 --> 00:23:21.250
<v Speaker 2>The office inherently forbids this.

384
00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:24.850
<v Speaker 1>Which makes the final step of this doctrine incredibly severe.

385
00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:25.570
<v Speaker 2>It does.

386
00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:35.850
<v Speaker 1>To believe that the leader of the time has acted without wisdom is not just a small localized error of judgment about one specific political decision.

387
00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:37.130
<v Speaker 2>No, it's much bigger than that.

388
00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:41.490
<v Speaker 1>It is to declare that this individual has fundamentally departed from wisdom entirely.

389
00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:46.210
<v Speaker 2>And whoever has departed from wisdom has by that very fact departed from the domain of his office.

390
00:23:46.690 --> 00:23:49.330
<v Speaker 2>The office simply is the seat of wisdom in the world.

391
00:23:49.810 --> 00:23:52.930
<v Speaker 2>A wisdomless leader is a literal contradiction in terms.

392
00:23:53.170 --> 00:23:58.050
<v Speaker 2>To depart from that domain is to depart from the faith itself, since the faith is entirely built upon that seat.

393
00:23:58.050 --> 00:23:59.010
<v Speaker 1>That's a huge claim.

394
00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:03.810
<v Speaker 2>It is, an objection that feels like ordinary healthy criticism saying this command makes no sense.

395
00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:10.370
<v Speaker 2>Turns out, when followed to its dimest root, to be a complete denial of the whole structure the believer claims to stand inside.

396
00:24:11.090 --> 00:24:14.810
<v Speaker 1>The author presses this through the images of severe guides, doesn't he?

397
00:24:14.810 --> 00:24:16.130
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, he uses a few.

398
00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:31.490
<v Speaker 1>Like the teacher who drives students through deeply resented, exhausting lessons, knowing they will thank him when they mature, or the physician who forces bitter medicine or even lancing and cauterization on a patient who cannot yet see that the agonizing pain is actually the cure.

399
00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:35.090
<v Speaker 2>The severity is a difficult mercy, cutting only to heal.

400
00:24:35.250 --> 00:24:37.650
<v Speaker 1>A wise agent cannot act pointlessly.

401
00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:43.890
<v Speaker 1>There is no such thing as an act that is truly empty, no matter how void it appears from your vantage point below.

402
00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:47.570
